WORKBENCH
=========

Workbench is a local-first AI workstation designed to provide a unified
interface for multiple specialized AI models and autonomous coursework
workflows.

The system is built around llama.cpp for local inference, a FastAPI
backend for orchestration and routing, and a gateway that provides a
single access point for the frontend and backend services.


CURRENT ARCHITECTURE
====================

                         Workbench
                            |
                            v
                    Gateway :8081
                            |
                 +----------+----------+
                 |                     |
                 v                     v
          FastAPI Backend          llama.cpp
             :8000                  :8080
                 |                     |
                 |                     +----------------+
                 |                                      |
                 v                                      v
          Model Routing                          Model Router
                 |                                      |
                 +----------------+---------------------+
                                  |
                    +-------------+-------------+
                    |             |             |
                    v             v             v
                 Coding       Reasoning      General
                Gemma 4 12B    Qwen3 4B      Gemma 4 E2B


SERVICES
========

1. llama.cpp

Location:

    llama.cpp/

llama.cpp is included as a Git submodule and provides the local inference
server and model router.

The current server runs on:

    100.72.139.99:8080

The server is configured to use physical GPUs 0 and 2.

Physical GPU 1 is intentionally not used by Workbench.

Current model routing:

    coding         -> Gemma 4 12B
    reasoning      -> Qwen3 4B
    general        -> Gemma 4 E2B
    document_image -> Gemma 4 12B


2. FastAPI Backend

Location:

    backend/

The backend provides the Workbench application logic.

Responsibilities currently include:

    - Chat API
    - Task analysis
    - Model routing
    - Model configuration
    - Document/image input detection
    - Communication with llama.cpp

The backend runs on:

    127.0.0.1:8000


3. Gateway

Location:

    backend/app/gateway.py

The gateway provides the external HTTP entry point for Workbench.

The gateway runs on:

    100.72.139.99:8081

Routing behavior:

    /v1/chat/completions
        -> FastAPI backend

    /v1/chat/completions/control
        -> FastAPI backend

    Other llama.cpp endpoints
        -> llama.cpp :8080

The gateway does not provide model-selection logic. Model selection
belongs to the backend and llama.cpp router.


MODEL CONFIGURATION
===================

There are two separate configuration layers.

1. llama.cpp configuration:

    models.ini

This defines the llama.cpp router's model presets and loading behavior.

2. Workbench configuration:

    backend/app/config/models.yaml

This defines Workbench's logical model roles.

Example logical roles:

    coding
    reasoning
    general
    document_image

The local models.yaml file is intentionally ignored by Git because
model configuration can be machine-specific.

A template/example configuration should be maintained separately.


CURRENT MODELS
==============

Gemma 4 12B
------------

Purpose:

    - Coding
    - Code generation
    - Debugging
    - Document/image understanding

Gemma 4 E2B
-----------

Purpose:

    - General tasks
    - Writing
    - Summarization
    - Task analysis

Qwen3 4B
--------

Purpose:

    - Reasoning
    - Problem solving
    - Analysis

Chandra OCR 2
-------------

Purpose:

    - OCR
    - Document extraction

Chandra is currently maintained as a separate model and is not yet
integrated into the main Workbench document pipeline.


DOCUMENT AND IMAGE INPUT
========================

Workbench can detect document/image input before normal task analysis.

Currently supported input types include:

    - Images
    - PDFs

Images are detected from OpenAI-compatible image_url message parts.

PDFs can be converted by the llama.cpp WebUI into image inputs.

When document/image input is detected:

    User input
        |
        v
    Document/Image detector
        |
        v
    document_image route
        |
        v
    Gemma 4 12B


For normal text-only requests:

    User input
        |
        v
    TaskAnalyzer
        |
        v
    ModelRouter
        |
        +---- coding ------> Gemma 4 12B
        |
        +---- reasoning ---> Qwen3 4B
        |
        +---- general -----> Gemma 4 E2B


TASK ANALYZER
=============

The TaskAnalyzer uses Gemma 4 E2B to classify text-only requests.

It determines the logical task category before the request reaches the
normal model router.

Current categories include:

    coding
    reasoning
    general


LLAMA CLIENT
============

The backend communicates with llama.cpp through an OpenAI-compatible
HTTP API.

Primary endpoint:

    /v1/chat/completions

The backend supports both:

    - Non-streaming responses
    - Streaming responses


GATEWAY
=======

The gateway exists so that the frontend can use a single endpoint while
Workbench decides whether a request should go to FastAPI or directly to
llama.cpp.

The gateway currently runs separately from the llama.cpp server.

It is intentionally not implemented as a traditional llama.cpp
reverse-proxy configuration. It is a dedicated FastAPI gateway.


NETWORK
=======

Current endpoints:

    llama.cpp
        http://100.72.139.99:8080

    gateway
        http://100.72.139.99:8081

    backend
        http://127.0.0.1:8000

Port 9000 is NOT used by Workbench.

Port 9000 is already occupied by another service on the shared server
and must not be modified.


GPU USAGE
=========

Workbench currently exposes:

    Physical GPU 0
    Physical GPU 2

through:

    CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="0,2"

The mapping is:

    CUDA logical GPU 0 -> physical GPU 0
    CUDA logical GPU 1 -> physical GPU 2

Physical GPU 1 is intentionally excluded.

Other users' GPU processes must not be modified.


MODEL WEIGHTS
=============

Model weights are stored under:

    models/

GGUF model files are intentionally ignored by Git.

Model weights must be downloaded separately.

The repository does not contain the actual model weights.


LLAMA.CPP SUBMODULE
===================

llama.cpp is maintained as a Git submodule:

    llama.cpp/

The repository currently tracks the submodule source rather than
committing the llama.cpp source directly into Workbench.

The llama.cpp build directory and compiled binaries are local artifacts
and are not tracked by Git.


PYTHON ENVIRONMENT
==================

Workbench uses uv for Python environment management.

Create the environment:

    uv venv

Activate it:

    source .venv/bin/activate

Install dependencies:

    uv sync


RUNNING THE SYSTEM
==================

Start llama.cpp first.

Example:

    CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="0,2" \
    LD_LIBRARY_PATH=~/projects/snap-ai/llama.cpp/build/bin \
    ~/projects/snap-ai/llama.cpp/build/bin/llama-server \
        --models-dir ~/projects/snap-ai/models \
        --models-preset ~/projects/snap-ai/models.ini \
        --models-max 3 \
        --host 100.72.139.99 \
        --port 8080 \
        --parallel 1 \
        --jinja \
        --webui-mcp-proxy


Start the FastAPI backend:

    cd backend

    ../.venv/bin/uvicorn app.main:app \
        --host 127.0.0.1 \
        --port 8000


Start the gateway:

    cd backend

    ../.venv/bin/uvicorn app.gateway:app \
        --host 100.72.139.99 \
        --port 8081


ENVIRONMENT
===========

Environment configuration is stored in:

    .env

A template is provided as:

    .env.example

The .env file is intentionally ignored by Git.

The application resolves the project-level .env independently of the
current working directory.


SECURITY
========

Workbench runs on a shared server.

The following rules are important:

    - Do not modify services belonging to other users.
    - Do not use physical GPU 1.
    - Do not use port 9000.
    - Do not expose model weights through the backend.
    - Do not commit .env files.
    - Do not commit GGUF model weights.
    - Do not give FastAPI unrestricted host access.
    - Generated code must not execute inside FastAPI.


SANDBOX EXECUTION
=================

A dedicated sandbox execution system is planned for running generated
and student code.

The intended architecture is:

    FastAPI
        |
        v
    Sandbox Service
        |
        v
    Isolated Execution Environment
        |
        +---- compile
        |
        +---- execute
        |
        v
    Result

The sandbox must provide:

    - No network access
    - CPU limits
    - Memory limits
    - Execution timeout
    - Process limits
    - Output limits
    - Temporary filesystem
    - No access to user home directories
    - No access to SSH credentials
    - No access to model files
    - No access to .env
    - Disposable execution environments

Generated code must never be executed directly by FastAPI on the host.


ASSIGNMENT AGENT
===============

Workbench is intended to evolve into an autonomous coursework and
assignment agent.

The planned workflow is:

    Assignment
        |
        v
    Perception
        |
        v
    Structured understanding
        |
        v
    Task planning
        |
        v
    Code/content generation
        |
        v
    Sandbox execution
        |
        v
    Test and verification
        |
        v
    Repair
        |
        v
    Final artifact


